SELECT 'Claude Cowork for Email Marketing: Segmentation, Sequences and Tests You Will Actually Run', 'claude-cowork-email-marketing-sequences', CONCAT('Email still returns more per dollar than any other channel, and most businesses send one newsletter to everyone because segmentation and sequences are too much work. Claude Cowork removes that constraint ', CHAR(0xE2,0x80,0x94 USING utf8mb4), ' personalised content per segment, full nurture sequences, and A/B tests you actually get round to running. Plus the WSQ course, funded up to 70%.'),
  '<p><strong>Short version:</strong> Email remains the highest-return channel most businesses own outright &mdash; no algorithm between you and the reader, no rented audience. Yet the majority send one undifferentiated newsletter to their whole list, because doing it properly means segmenting the audience, writing different content per segment, building multi-step sequences and testing them, which is more work than anyone has. Claude Cowork removes that constraint: the segmentation analysis, the per-segment copy, the five-email sequence and the test variants all become an afternoon rather than a fortnight. This article covers the workflow and the hygiene that keeps it out of spam folders. The hands-on version is the <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-claude-cowork-for-email-marketing.html">WSQ Claude Cowork for Email Marketing</a> course (TGS-2020503109), funded up to 70%.</p> <h2>Why the single-newsletter habit persists</h2> <p>Every email marketer knows segmented, personalised email outperforms a broadcast. The reason it does not happen is arithmetic. Four segments times a five-email sequence is twenty pieces of copy, plus subject lines, plus variants to test &mdash; call it a fortnight of work for one campaign. Against a deadline, sending one email to everyone is the rational choice, and so it becomes the permanent choice.</p> <p>That is the constraint AI actually removes. Not &ldquo;writing an email faster&rdquo;, which was never the problem, but making the twenty-piece version cost about what the one-piece version used to. Once that is true, the strategy your team already knows is correct becomes affordable.</p> <h2>Segmentation: start with behaviour, not demographics</h2> <p>Useful segments are built on what people did, because behaviour predicts what they want next far better than job title does. A practical starting set:</p> <table> <thead><tr><th>Segment</th><th>Signal</th><th>What they need to hear</th></tr></thead> <tbody> <tr><td>New subscribers</td><td>Joined in the last 30 days</td><td>Who you are, what to expect, one quick win</td></tr> <tr><td>Engaged non-buyers</td><td>Opens and clicks, no purchase</td><td>The objection that is holding them &mdash; price, timing, doubt</td></tr> <tr><td>Customers</td><td>Bought at least once</td><td>Getting more value, the next logical step</td></tr> <tr><td>Lapsed</td><td>No opens in 90 days</td><td>A re-engagement attempt, then a clean removal</td></tr> </tbody> </table> <p>Four segments is enough to beat a broadcast substantially. Connected to your customer data through <a href="https://modelcontextprotocol.io/">MCP tools</a>, the workspace can propose segments from actual behaviour rather than guesses &mdash; and, more usefully, tell you how many people are in each, which is how you find out that the segment you were about to write four emails for contains nine people.</p> <h2>Sequences: where the revenue actually is</h2> <p>A welcome sequence is the highest-value email asset most businesses do not have. It runs forever, it reaches people at peak interest, and it is written once. The same is true of a nurture sequence for people who enquired but did not buy.</p> <p>Generate the sequence as a set, not one email at a time, so the arc holds together:</p> <pre>Write a five-email welcome sequence for new subscribers who&#10;downloaded the course outline.&#10;&#10;Arc: (1) welcome + what to expect, (2) the most common mistake&#10;we see, (3) a client result with a real number, (4) the funding&#10;options explained simply, (5) a direct invitation to register.&#10;&#10;Rules: one call to action per email; subject lines under 45&#10;characters; no exclamation marks; do not repeat the same opening&#10;formula; anything you cannot support from the brief, flag it.&#10;&#10;Reference: brand-voice.md, our three best-performing emails.</pre> <p>The instruction not to repeat the opening formula matters more than it sounds. The default failure is five emails that all begin the same way, which is the clearest signal to a reader that nobody is really writing to them.</p> <h2>A/B testing you will actually get round to</h2> <p>Most teams intend to test and do not, because generating genuine variants is tedious. Ask for five subject lines built on <em>different mechanisms</em> &mdash; curiosity, benefit, urgency, question, plain statement &mdash; rather than five rewordings, and you have a real test. The same for a call to action or an opening paragraph.</p> <p>Two disciplines keep the results honest: change one thing at a time, and wait for enough conversions rather than enough opens. Open rates have become an unreliable metric with privacy-protection features inflating them, so judge on clicks and conversions where you can.</p> <h2>Turning the good campaign into a Skill</h2> <p>The first time you build a segmented sequence with AI help, it takes an afternoon. The second time it should take twenty minutes, and the difference is whether you captured the process. A custom Skill does that: it stores the structure that worked, the voice rules, the constraints and the examples, so the next campaign starts from your proven pattern rather than a blank prompt.</p> <p>What belongs in an email Skill:</p> <ul> <li><strong>The arc that works for your audience</strong> &mdash; not a generic five-email template, but the sequence shape your list responded to, with a note on why each step exists.</li> <li><strong>Your voice rules and your banned phrases.</strong> Every team has tells it wants avoided; write them down once.</li> <li><strong>The structural constraints</strong> &mdash; subject-line length, one call to action, link conventions, the sign-off format.</li> <li><strong>Two or three real winners and one failure,</strong> with a line on what went wrong. The failure teaches more than the winners do.</li> </ul> <p>The wider benefit is that email quality stops depending on who is free that week. A new team member invoking the Skill produces something structurally correct on their first attempt, and the review conversation becomes about the idea rather than the format &mdash; which is the conversation worth having.</p> <h2>Deliverability and consent: the unglamorous half</h2> <p>None of the above matters if the email lands in spam or should not have been sent at all. In Singapore, marketing email falls under the <a href="https://www.pdpc.gov.sg/overview-of-pdpa/the-legislation/personal-data-protection-act">PDPA</a>, including its provisions on consent and unsubscribe handling; the PDPC''s <a href="https://www.pdpc.gov.sg/guidelines-and-consultation">advisory guidelines</a> are the reference. The operational rules:</p> <ul> <li><strong>Send only to people who opted in,</strong> and keep the record of when and how.</li> <li><strong>Honour unsubscribes immediately</strong> and make the link obvious. A hidden unsubscribe produces spam complaints, which cost you far more than the lost subscriber.</li> <li><strong>Clean the list.</strong> Repeatedly emailing people who never open trains inbox providers to filter you. Remove them rather than hoping.</li> <li><strong>Keep personalisation proportionate.</strong> Referencing a purchase is helpful; demonstrating how much you know about someone is unsettling. The line is whether the reader would be comfortable knowing why they got this email.</li> </ul> <h2>Learn it hands-on, funded up to 70%</h2> <p>The <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-claude-cowork-for-email-marketing.html">WSQ &ndash; Claude Cowork for Email Marketing</a> course (TGS-2020503109) is a two-day programme covering the full workflow. Its five topics:</p> <ol> <li><strong>Email marketing strategy and campaign planning with Claude Cowork</strong> &mdash; audience research and strategy development.</li> <li><strong>Audience management, segmentation and personalisation</strong> &mdash; subscriber segments and content for different profiles and journey stages.</li> <li><strong>Creating email content and campaigns with custom Claude Skills</strong> &mdash; subject lines, promotional emails, newsletters, welcome sequences, nurture campaigns, announcements and re-engagement, built as reusable Skills.</li> <li><strong>Email marketing automation with Claude Cowork and MCP tools</strong> &mdash; connecting customer data and marketing systems, with human oversight, data privacy and compliance.</li> <li><strong>Campaign analytics, A/B testing and performance optimisation</strong> &mdash; metrics, tests, engagement trends and recommendations.</li> </ol> <p>Assessment is a written and a practical exam.</p> <table> <thead><tr><th>Who</th><th>Funding</th><th>What you pay (incl. 9% GST)</th></tr></thead> <tbody> <tr><td>Full course fee</td><td>&ndash;</td><td>S$720.00 before GST (S$64.80 GST)</td></tr> <tr><td>Singapore Citizens and PRs aged 21 and above</td><td>50% WSQ funding</td><td><strong>S$424.80</strong></td></tr> <tr><td>Singapore Citizens aged 40 and above (MCES), or SME-sponsored SG/PR staff</td><td>70% WSQ funding</td><td><strong>S$280.80</strong></td></tr> </tbody> </table> <p>Eligible Singaporeans can offset the nett fee with <a href="https://www.skillsfuture.gov.sg/">SkillsFuture Credit</a> or PSEA, and eligible Singapore-registered companies can tap SkillsFuture Enterprise Credit (SFEC); SME employers can also claim absentee payroll. The course runs over two full days (9:30am to 6:30pm), with physical classroom and synchronous online (Zoom) options. Fees are as published on the course page at the time of writing &mdash; the live page always carries the current fee and the next available dates.</p> <p>For the agent-driven version with autonomous campaign coordination, see <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/casl-agentic-ai-for-email-marketing-campaign.html">CASL Agentic AI for Email Marketing Campaign</a>; for the cross-channel workspace, <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-claude-cowork-for-digital-marketing.html">WSQ Claude Cowork for Digital Marketing</a>.</p> <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2> <div class="mmd-faq"> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">Is email marketing still worth it?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>Yes, and for most businesses it is the best-returning channel they own outright &mdash; no algorithm between you and the reader, no rented audience. The problem is not the channel; it is that doing it properly has been too labour-intensive for small teams to sustain.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">How many segments should I start with?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>Four is plenty: new subscribers, engaged non-buyers, customers, and lapsed. Build them on behaviour rather than demographics, and check the size of each before writing for it &mdash; a segment of nine people does not need its own five-email sequence.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">What should I write first if I have nothing?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>A welcome sequence. It reaches people at peak interest, it runs forever once written, and it is the highest-value email asset most businesses lack. A nurture sequence for enquirers who did not buy is the natural second.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">How do I A/B test email properly?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>Generate variants that use different mechanisms &mdash; curiosity, benefit, urgency, question, plain statement &mdash; rather than rewordings. Change one element at a time and judge on clicks and conversions rather than opens, since privacy features have made open rates unreliable.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">What are the PDPA rules for marketing email in Singapore?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>Send only to people who consented, keep the record of that consent, honour unsubscribes promptly and make the link easy to find. Personal data used for personalisation is covered by the PDPA, and the PDPC&rsquo;s advisory guidelines are the reference point.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">Do I need a big list for this to be worth learning?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>No. Segmentation and sequences often matter more at small scale, because every subscriber is a larger share of the opportunity. The techniques work on a list of 500 as well as 50,000.</p></div> </details> </div> <h2>The bottom line</h2> <p>Everyone knows segmented, sequenced, tested email beats a monthly broadcast. What stopped it was the labour, and that constraint has genuinely changed. Build four behavioural segments, write the welcome sequence first, test variants that differ in mechanism rather than wording, and keep the consent and list hygiene disciplined. The strategy is not new &mdash; it is just finally affordable.</p> <p><strong>Ready to send email that works?</strong> <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-claude-cowork-for-email-marketing.html">Register for WSQ &ndash; Claude Cowork for Email Marketing</a> &mdash; 2 days, funded up to 70%, SkillsFuture Credit claimable.</p>',
  'Dr. Alfred Ang', 1, '2025-06-23', 'TGS-2020503109', 'TGS-2020503109',
  'Claude Cowork for Email Marketing: Sequences, Segments, A/B Tests | WSQ Course',
  CONCAT('Use Claude Cowork for email marketing ', CHAR(0xE2,0x80,0x94 USING utf8mb4), ' audience segmentation, personalised content, welcome and nurture sequences, MCP-connected automation and A/B testing ', CHAR(0xE2,0x80,0x94 USING utf8mb4), ' with the WSQ Claude Cowork for Email Marketing course (TGS-2020503109) funded up to 70%.'),
  'claude cowork email marketing, ai email marketing, email segmentation ai, nurture sequence ai, ai subject lines, email automation singapore, wsq email marketing course, skillsfuture email marketing, ab testing email ai, email personalisation ai, newsletter ai, email marketing course singapore',
  129, 'manual-skip', 'manual-skip', NOW(), NOW()
